- How would you adapt for non-byte-aligned lanes?\n- How would you verify correctness with minimal tests?

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to 64-bit inputs?
- Compare this approach to using hardware popcount instructions in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive bit-run counts without loops, handling 128-bit arithmetic and portable popcounts.

## Key Concepts

- Detect run starts with mask m = x & ~(x << 1).
- Implement 128-bit left shift across hi/lo: (hi,lo) << 1 becomes (hi<<1 | lo>>63, lo<<1).
- Compute popcount on a 128-bit value by summing 64-bit SWAR popcounts on hi and lo.

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like pseudocode
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 shl1(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63); r.lo = x.lo << 1; return r; }
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56; }
unsigned countRuns128(uint128 x){
  uint128 t = shl1(x);
  uint128 m; m.hi = x.hi & ~t.hi; m.lo = x.lo & ~t.lo;
  return popcount64(m.hi) + popcount64(m.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage hardware intrinsics for popcount on supported platforms?
- How would you scale the approach to 256-bit or 512-bit widths?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to also return the index of the isolated bit in a 0..127 range?
- What are the edge-case behaviors when x is zero or has a single 1 at the MSB?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

