## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits is a practical pattern in spatial indexing (Morton codes) and graphics. This tests branch-free bit twiddling and understanding of bit spreading.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (part1by1)
- Masked shifts and combiners
- Branch-free composition for 64-bit results

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t spread32(uint32_t x){
  uint64_t y = x;
  y = (y | (y << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  y = (y | (y << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  y = (y | (y << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = (y | (y << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  y = (y | (y << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return y;
}
uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t a, uint32_t b){
  return spread32(a) | (spread32(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs to a 128-bit output?
- What intrinsics would you use on your platform?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

